[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 986,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 951,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150153,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 951,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Perineurioma is a benign unencapsulated peripheral nerve sheath tumour composed entirely of cells with perineurial differentiation.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 987,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 952,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150154,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 952,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>95710/0 Perineurioma, NOS</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 988,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 953,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150155,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 953,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>2E92.4Y &amp; XH0XF7 Perineurioma, NOS, of large intestine</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 989,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 954,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150156,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 954,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> benign fibroblastic polyp.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 990,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 955,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150157,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 955,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 991,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 956,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150158,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 956,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Most gastrointestinal perineuriomas occur in the large bowel, with only rare occurrences in the small bowel or stomach.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 992,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 957,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150159,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 957,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Most intestinal perineuriomas are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally during colorectal cancer screening. The rectosigmoid colon is the most common site. Occasionally, the tumours can present with recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding (gastric location).</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 993,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 958,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150160,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 958,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Gastrointestinal perineuriomas occur rarely in the GI tract and have been reported in middle-aged adults, with a female predominance among published cases {40225020}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 994,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 959,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150161,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 959,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 995,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 960,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150162,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 960,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Similar to schwannomas, most perineuriomas harbour chromosome&nbsp;22 deletions or monosomies, probably targeting the <em>NF2</em> tumour suppressor gene. The molecular pathogenetic basis for colorectal mucosal perineuriomas is unknown, although associated serrated polyps harbour typical <em>BRAF</em> p.Val600Glu (V600E) mutations in the epithelial component {20962618;21836484}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1002,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 967,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150163,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 967,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Most gastrointestinal perineuriomas present as small (2&ndash;6&nbsp;mm) colonic polyps. Less commonly, they can present as non-polypoid lesions within the submucosa where they are usually larger, often between 30&nbsp;and 50&nbsp;mm. The submucosal tumours are usually well circumscribed, white, solid, and may appear myxoid.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 996,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 961,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150164,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 961,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Intramucosal polypoid perineuriomas expand the lamina propria and uniformly entrap crypts. Colorectal mucosal perineuriomas are often associated with serrated epithelial polyps, including both hyperplastic polyps and sessile serrated lesions {15958849;21836484;23588369}. Submucosal perineuriomas appear circumscribed but can focally infiltrate into the muscularis propria and subserosa {23588369}. Similar to perineurioma of soft tissues, the gastrointestinal tumours are composed of uniform spindle cells with ovoid to elongated and tapering nuclei, as well as bipolar cytoplasmic processes. The cells are generally arranged in a diffuse solid growth, often showing a distinctive whorled architecture around crypts. Mitoses are rare or absent, and there is no necrosis. The cells are positive for EMA (highlighting delicate cell processes), claudin-1, and/or GLUT1 {15958849}. A subset of cases also express CD34. Gastrointestinal perineuriomas are negative for S100, GFAP, KIT, SMA, and keratins.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 997,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 962,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150165,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 962,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 998,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 963,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150166,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 963,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1001,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 966,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150167,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 966,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> uniform spindle cells with distinctive whorled architecture in mucosa or submucosa.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positive for EMA; negative for S100.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 999,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 964,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150168,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 964,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1000,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 965,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150169,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 177,
        "headingId": 965,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Gastrointestinal perineuriomas are benign.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:26"
      }
    ],
